  "full_response": "Git's branching and merging mechanisms are fundamental to its distributed version control model. Here's a detailed look at how Git handles them internally:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Branching in Git**\nBranching in Git is lightweight and efficient because it doesn't copy files or directories. Instead, it creates a new **pointer** to a commit.\n\n#### **How Git Branches Work Internally:**\n- A **branch** is simply a **movable pointer** to a commit (stored as a file in `.git/refs/heads/`).\n  - Example: The `main` branch is stored in `.git/refs/heads/main` as a 40-character SHA-1 hash of the commit it points to.\n- When you create a new branch (`git branch feature`), Git:\n  1. Creates a new file in `.git/refs/heads/feature` containing the current commit's hash.\n  2. Updates `HEAD` (a special pointer) to reference the new branch (if you switch to it with `git checkout` or `git switch`).\n\n#### **Key Points:**\n- Branches are **cheap** (just a pointer, no file duplication).\n- Switching branches (`git checkout`) updates the working directory to match the commit the branch points to.\n- Git uses **packfiles** and **deltas** to store changes efficiently, so branching doesn’t consume much disk space.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Merging in Git**\nMerging combines changes from one branch into another. Git uses **three-way merging** (comparing common ancestors) to resolve differences.\n\n#### **How Git Merges Work Internally:**\n1. **Find the Merge Base:**\n   - Git identifies the **common ancestor** (the most recent commit shared by both branches) using the commit history.\n   - Example: If merging `feature` into `main`, Git finds the last commit where `main` and `feature` diverged.\n\n2. **Three-Way Merge:**\n   - Git compares:\n     - The **merge base** (common ancestor).\n     - The **tip of the current branch** (`main`).\n     - The **tip of the branch being merged** (`feature`).\n   - It generates a **merge commit** that combines changes from both branches.\n\n3. **Merge Strategies:**\n   - **Recursive (default):** For most cases, Git uses a recursive merge strategy to handle criss-cross merges.\n   - **Octopus:** For merging multiple branches at once.\n",
